WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.736
<v New Human Services Supervisor>I finished the series, from the orientation through designing services.

00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:07.472
<v New Human Services Supervisor>I see barriers everywhere now. Honestly, it is a little

00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:11.208
<v New Human Services Supervisor>discouraging. Most of the rules are not mine to change.

00:00:11.558 --> 00:00:15.032
<v Division Director>That is a common feeling. Start with one barrier. What

00:00:15.032 --> 00:00:18.507
<v Division Director>can you actually observe, not what you think is causing

00:00:18.507 --> 00:00:18.854
<v Division Director>it?

00:00:19.204 --> 00:00:22.751
<v New Human Services Supervisor>Openings for acting lead roles in my unit get announced

00:00:22.751 --> 00:00:25.588
<v New Human Services Supervisor>in the morning huddle. Evening staff rarely apply.

00:00:25.938 --> 00:00:29.718
<v Division Director>Good. That is an observation. Do you know why they

00:00:29.718 --> 00:00:30.474
<v Division Director>rarely apply?

00:00:30.824 --> 00:00:34.047
<v New Human Services Supervisor>I assumed they were not interested. But the curiosity course

00:00:34.047 --> 00:00:35.336
<v New Human Services Supervisor>made me doubt that.

00:00:35.686 --> 00:00:39.526
<v Division Director>Right. Research on perspective taking found that imagining someone's view

00:00:39.526 --> 00:00:43.366
<v Division Director>does not make you more accurate. Asking does. So ask

00:00:43.366 --> 00:00:46.822
<v Division Director>them, and make it easy to answer in writing.

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:50.148
<v New Human Services Supervisor>My first instinct was to ask for a training for

00:00:50.148 --> 00:00:51.636
<v New Human Services Supervisor>the day-shift leads about fairness.

00:00:51.986 --> 00:00:55.918
<v Division Director>I understand. But a field experiment found a one-off training

00:00:55.918 --> 00:00:59.851
<v Division Director>changed attitudes without changing behavior for the people it most

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:03.783
<v Division Director>needed to reach. Studies of employers found that naming someone

00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:07.322
<v Division Director>responsible did more. Who owns how openings are announced?

00:01:07.672 --> 00:01:11.653
<v New Human Services Supervisor>Me, actually. The selection rules belong to human resources, but

00:01:11.653 --> 00:01:14.440
<v New Human Services Supervisor>how my unit announces openings is mine.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:18.099
<v Division Director>Then you have a test. For the next two openings,

00:01:18.099 --> 00:01:21.408
<v Division Director>announce on both shifts and in writing. Predict what will

00:01:21.408 --> 00:01:24.717
<v Division Director>happen. Track how many people on each shift saw the

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:25.710
<v Division Director>notice in time.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.030
<v New Human Services Supervisor>And if it is mixed? Say more evening staff apply

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:30.812
<v New Human Services Supervisor>but they still are not selected?

00:01:31.162 --> 00:01:35.149
<v Division Director>Then you report exactly that. You ask why, and you

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:39.136
<v Division Director>bring the evidence to the person or area responsible for

00:01:39.136 --> 00:01:43.123
<v Division Director>selection. Most published improvement projects stop after one try. Do

00:01:43.123 --> 00:01:43.522
<v Division Director>not.

00:01:43.872 --> 00:01:47.256
<v New Human Services Supervisor>So the series gave me the knowledge. The change comes

00:01:47.256 --> 00:01:50.640
<v New Human Services Supervisor>from a named owner, a small test and honest follow-up.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.278
<v Division Director>Exactly. And when a newer leader tells you they cannot

00:01:54.278 --> 00:01:57.566
<v Division Director>change the rule, you will know what to ask them.
